2f7c857cf6bd"} {"question" "q-330903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(-1.55<Z<1.55).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/45d71abed85d91a5a1b150fb741bcc8fa9967f7b2b8d77df35423a909b1e8522.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.55<Z<1.55) & =P(Z<1.55)-P(Z\\le -1.55) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.55)-P(Z\\le -1.55) \\\\  & =Φ(1.55)-Φ(-1.55)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.55)=0.9394,\n$$\n\nbut we are not given $Φ(-1.55).$ However, we can compute this using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.55) & =P(Z\\le -1.55) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 1.55) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.55) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.55) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.55) \\\\  & =1-0.9394 \\\\  & =0.0606\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.55<Z<1.55) & =Φ(1.55)-Φ(-1.55) \\\\  & =0.9394-0.0606 \\\\  & =0.8788.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/state.json" "file_sha256" "f38b28e68eb1c72046b151d63c599d8baf82ed4ee0a6819917072f7c857cf6bd"} {"question" "q-330903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9675d43953465dc0c2310694be85278d058ad772405778d09e3fc4335832b01f"} {"question" "q-330903" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/state.json" "file_sha256" "f38b28e68eb1c72046b151d63c599d8baf82ed4ee0a6819917072f7c857cf6bd"} {"question" "q-330903" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.8788"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/state.json" "file_sha256" "f38b28e68eb1c72046b151d63c599d8baf82ed4ee0a6819917072f7c857cf6bd"} {"question" "e-13270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z \\gt -0.76).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8051 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8078 \\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/example-13270.json" "file_sha256" "d7b03dedb344dbc5c6b4499867f53308b3a0f2f0ad9d5fd9b68a1814a5629198"} {"question" "e-13270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/f88bf9a66694858e9eaf16f616555eda6d9da7de9f62b678bc1f03b32bf9a620.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt -a) = P(Z \\lt a) = \\Phi(a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P( Z > -0.76).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/fbacbbe3f53c73316fd070d95e4c077cd695bdcf884d30a95f6b0850df328c80.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\lt 0.76).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/c0f612336c02eee97d6c66b56f0854286b3a23b90053810e9e4fb75b698b5a60.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > -0.76)\n= P(Z < 0.76)\n= \\Phi(0.76)\n= 0.7764.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/example-13270.json" "file_sha256" "d7b03dedb344dbc5c6b4499867f53308b3a0f2f0ad9d5fd9b68a1814a5629198"} {"question" "e-13271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z \\lt -1.54).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9066 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9082 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9099 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9222 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9236 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9251 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9370 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9382 \\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/example-13271.json" "file_sha256" "6ef5a696adfc153abbe65be245cb43ecf9da50e2d67ac386763d6e1ff1883ecb"} {"question" "e-13271" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/4d6524ff1e32b2c2b4aaf7481dbf63a96f847cd91ce66aeeb58690f37bbe50e5.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt a) = P(Z \\lt -a) = \\Phi(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P( Z \\lt -1.54).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/52d4c1add91819ff1f9dddf609b5753506c26e6cd6ee7f3cb14150ccb9b60971.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\gt 1.54).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/7fef056bb5564986a009198685a2951de87ccb8e09c932c6902bfafdcdf0656f.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-1.54) & =P(Z>1.54) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.54) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.54) \\\\  & =1-0.9382 \\\\  & =0.0618.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/example-13271.json" "file_sha256" "6ef5a696adfc153abbe65be245cb43ecf9da50e2d67ac386763d6e1ff1883ecb"} {"question" "e-13269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ compute $P(-0.53 <   Z <        0.53).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6628 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.67 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6985 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7324 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/example-13269.json" "file_sha256" "30155842c3b8cea0d50d5b84e91f3a59e2e6bf22d1d18dcea81ec91036961cbd"} {"question" "e-13269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(-0.53 <        Z <        0.53).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/bb86bde3595d6a64d1cd82da3c928f28c496f495f9c6815e56ee025a4a69d685.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.53<Z<0.53) & =P(Z<0.53)-P(Z\\le -0.53) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.53)-P(Z\\le -0.53) \\\\  & =Φ(0.53)-Φ(-0.53)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.53) = 0.7019,\n$$\n\nbut we are not given $\\Phi(-0.53) .$ However, we can compute this using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.53) & =P(Z\\le -0.53) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.53) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.53) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.53) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.53) \\\\  & =1-0.7019 \\\\  & =0.2981\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.53<Z<0.53) & =Φ(0.53)-Φ(-0.53) \\\\  & =0.7019-0.2981 \\\\  & =0.4038.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/example-13269.json" "file_sha256" "30155842c3b8cea0d50d5b84e91f3a59e2e6bf22d1d18dcea81ec91036961cbd"}] "usage" {"e-13270" [] "e-13271" [] "e-13269" []} "basis" 2455 "retractions" [[17592186198111 :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(Z \\gt -0.76)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8051 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8078 \\,$ |"] [17592186198111 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/66fefa957dd168d1589cdb34bce3bced.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt -a) = P(Z \\lt a) = \\Phi(a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(Z > -0.76)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/1bc0f3dd71234ca7671872a937519a38.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\lt 0.76)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/bd8ed379c32619927570445941878c4f.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > -0.76) = P(Z < 0.76) = \\Phi(0.76) = 0.7764\n$$"] [17592186198112 :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(Z \\lt -1.54)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9066 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9082 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9099 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9222 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9236 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9251 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9370 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9382 \\,$ |"] [17592186198112 :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/95c129d15152dcce16214fa0613e8fb6.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt a) = P(Z \\lt -a) = \\Phi(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(Z \\lt -1.54)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/aa5d3354086f3622c4f7c5920b1753b8.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\gt 1.54)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/3e0d56a379a7fecf3403b9750a7133ab.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Z < - 1.54) &= P(Z > 1.54) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z < 1.54) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(1.54) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.9382 \\\\\n&= 0.0618\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198110 :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(-0.53 < Z < 0.53)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6628 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.67 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6985 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7324 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ |"] [17592186198110 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(-0.53 < Z < 0.53)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/0b30d484a778ffede28d541415b21e58.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$,\n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z)\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.53 < Z < 0.53) &= P(Z < 0.53) - P(Z \\le - 0.53) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 0.53) - P(Z \\le - 0.53) \\\\\n&= Φ(0.53) - Φ(-0.53)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.53) = 0.7019\n$$\n\nbut we are not given $\\Phi(-0.53)$. However, we can compute this using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ(-0.53) &= P(Z \\le - 0.53) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\ge 0.53) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z < 0.53) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z \\le 0.53) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(0.53) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.7019 \\\\\n&= 0.2981\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.53 < Z < 0.53) &= Φ(0.53) - Φ(-0.53) \\\\\n&= 0.7019 - 0.2981 \\\\\n&= 0.4038\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
